    <script type="application/ld+json">{"mainEntity":{"headline":"Decrements in Health-Related Quality of Life and Health-State Utility Value Due to Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus: Implications for Future Health Economic Evaluation","description":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) affects all age groups, including children and adolescents. However, middle-aged and older individuals are the most vulnerable, and T2DM has been a major contributor to the global disease burden for decades. While previous research has focused on clinical outcomes, such as cardiovascular diseases and complications, less attention has been paid to broader impacts, including health-related quality of life (HRQOL). This study investigated whether T2DM predicts HRQOL among middle-aged and older Australians. We used data from waves 9, 13, 17, and 21 of the Household, Income and Labour Dynamics in Australia (HILDA) Survey. The analysis included 27,564 person-year observations from 11,042 individuals. Fixed-effects regression models were employed to examine the association between T2DM and HRQOL. T2DM was negatively associated with HRQOL, particularly with the Physical Component Summary (PCS) and the SF-6D health-state utility value. Specifically, T2DM was associated with lower PCS scores (β = −1.01, 95% CI: −1.62 to −0.41) and lower SF-6D utility values (β = −0.0080, 95% CI: −0.0154 to −0.0007), whereas no significant association was observed with the Mental Component Summary (MCS). The negative associations with PCS and SF-6D were most evident among men and adults aged 60 years and older, with additional subgroup analyses indicating a greater physical HRQOL burden among participants with insufficient physical activity, disability, smoking, alcohol consumption, and selected income groups. T2DM was associated with poorer physical HRQOL and lower health-state utility over the 13-year follow-up, with the greatest burden observed among men, older adults, and several behavioural and health-related subgroups. These findings highlight the importance of incorporating HRQOL assessment into routine diabetes care and support targeted prevention and management strategies that promote healthy lifestyles, including regular physical activity, smoking cessation, and moderation of alcohol consumption. The estimated SF-6D utility decrements also provide evidence to inform future quality-adjusted life-year (QALY)-based economic evaluations of diabetes prevention and management interventions.","datePublished":"2026-09-15T00:00:00Z","dateModified":"2026-09-15T00:00:00Z","pageStart":"1","pageEnd":"28","sameAs":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11482-026-10672-w","keywords":["Australia","PCS","SF-6D utility value","T2DM","Quality of Life Research","Sociology","general","Political Science"],"image":["https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs11482-026-10672-w/MediaObjects/11482_2026_10672_Fig1_HTML.png"],"isPartOf":{"name":"Applied Research in Quality of Life","issn":["1871-2576","1871-2584"],"@type":["Periodical"]},"publisher":{"name":"Springer Netherlands","logo":{"url":"https://www.springernature.com/app-sn/public/images/logo-springernature.png","@type":"ImageObject"},"@type":"Organization"},"author":[{"name":"Addisu Shunu Beyene","url":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9706-7441","affiliation":[{"name":"The University of Newcastle","address":{"name":"School of Information and Physical Sciences, The University of Newcastle, Newcastle, Australia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"email":"addisushunu@gmail.com","@type":"Person"},{"name":"Tolassa Wakayo Ushula","affiliation":[{"name":"Deakin University","address":{"name":"Institute of Physical Activity and Nutrition, Faculty of Health, Deakin University, Melbourne, Australia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Hirbo Shore Roba","affiliation":[{"name":"Haramaya University","address":{"name":"School of Public Health, Haramaya University, Harar, Ethiopia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"},{"name":"University of Southern Queensland","address":{"name":"School of Public Health, University of Southern Queensland, Toowoomba, Australia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Khorshed Alam","affiliation":[{"name":"Haramaya University","address":{"name":"School of Public Health, Haramaya University, Harar, Ethiopia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"},{"name":"University of Southern Queensland","address":{"name":"School of Business, University of Southern Queensland, Toowoomba, Australia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Syed Afroz Keramat","affiliation":[{"name":"The University of Melbourne","address":{"name":"Child Health Economics Unit, Centre for Health Policy, The University of Melbourne, Melbourne, Australia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"},{"name":"Deakin University","address":{"name":"Deakin Trials Hub (DTH), Deakin University, Melbourne, Australia","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"}],"isAccessibleForFree":false,"hasPart":{"isAccessibleForFree":false,"cssSelector":".main-content","@type":"WebPageElement"},"@type":"ScholarlyArticle"},"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage"}</script>

        
        
